          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Darkness primarily symbolizes the broad complex of sin, evil, spiritual blindness, unbelief, and alienation from God, while also carrying several context-dependent secondary meanings.\",\"book_summary\":\"Darkness signifies disobedient deeds kept concealed from exposure by the light.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Moral and spiritual evil or sin in opposition to God and divine light.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Sin specifically insofar as it is concealed and avoids exposure.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts darkness to concealed disobedience, excluding or leaving untreated open wrongdoing and broader consensus cases such as spiritual ignorance, alienation from God, Satanic dominion, death, judgment, suffering, and divine hiddenness.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: concealed disobedience is a recognizable instance of the consensus category of sin and evil. The disagreement concerns scope and precision rather than replacement of the central referent.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus identification of darkness with moral evil but selects one particular mechanism emphasized in John 3:19–20: evildoers avoid the light so their deeds will not be exposed. This materially narrows the symbol’s extension and therefore constitutes refinement rather than a match or divergence.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"John 3:19–20 clearly associates darkness with evil deeds and avoidance of exposure, but it does not unambiguously identify darkness itself exclusively as concealed disobedience; concealment may be the reason evildoers prefer darkness rather than the complete symbolic meaning of darkness.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
